Hello everyone, today we will discuss how to achieve cost savings and risk mitigation during the engineering project design process. In English, this process is called Value Engineering, which is often closely tied to Peer Review.

Excellent design not only ensures smooth project execution but also helps owners save costs, reduce construction risks, and even provide greater convenience for later-stage project operations and maintenance.

This lecture will primarily cover the following content:

Exploring the criteria for excellent design;

Analyzing the concepts of peer review and value engineering;

Elaborating on the objectives and advantages of value engineering;

Introducing the methodology of value engineering;

Sharing personal case studies of value engineering in practice.

It is worth noting that value engineering costs typically account for 10% to 20% of design fees, while design fees themselves constitute 5% to 10% of the total project cost. For example, in a project worth 1 million yuan, the design fee would be approximately 50,000 yuan, with 20% (i.e., 10,000 yuan) allocated to value engineering. However, this investment can save the entire project at least 100,000 to 200,000 yuan in expenses. This demonstrates the significant cost-effectiveness of value engineering.

Below is the methodology and implementation process of Value Engineering.

Typically, the parties involved in Value Engineering include:
- The owner and their project manager, operation and maintenance team;
- The design department, primarily composed of architects and engineers;
- Peer-reviewing architects and engineers;
- And the construction manager.

During the peer review and value engineering process, we typically need to examine the following aspects:

First, verify whether the design complies with all codes and standards;
Second, confirm whether the design clearly defines the project’s scope of work;
Third, check whether the design meets all the owner’s requirements;
Fourth, assess whether the design presents any field construction issues, such as constructability challenges or excessive construction difficulty, which may lead to delays and additional costs;
Fifth, analyze the impact of the design on the MEP systems’ operation and maintenance after project completion;
Sixth, review whether the design contains any omissions or errors.
